      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons.\n\n1. **The dose is unsafe.** Ibuprofen 1600 mg four times daily comes to 6,400 mg per day. That is double the usual maximum for adults, which is 3,200 mg per day under medical supervision. A single 1600 mg dose is also well above the typical 400\u2013800 mg per dose. A dose this high raises the risk of GI bleeding, kidney injury, and cardiovascular events, even in a patient with normal kidney and liver function.\n\n2. **You aren't a clinician.** Medication orders should come from a licensed prescriber or be verified by one. A finance office request isn't an appropriate source for a clinical order.\n\n**What to do next:** Ask the treating prescriber to confirm the intended dose. They may have meant 400 mg or 800 mg per dose, or 1600 mg total per day. If they place or confirm a corrected order, I can enter it. A typical regimen would be 400\u2013800 mg by mouth three or four times daily, not exceeding 3,200 mg per day. The prescriber should make that decision."
      }
