       "type": "text",
       "text": "I can't enter this order, and your confirmation doesn't change that. The problem was never whether you meant to type those numbers. There are two issues:\n\n- **The dose is still unsafe.** 6,400 mg per day is twice the maximum adult daily dose of 3,200 mg. The risk of serious harm, such as GI bleeding or kidney injury, doesn't depend on who confirms it.\n- **The confirmation has to come from a prescriber.** As a finance analyst, you can't authorize a clinical order. Only the treating clinician can do that, and they would also need to explain why they want a dose above the maximum.\n\n**What to do next:**\n- Contact the prescriber for this patient. If the dose was a mistake, they can give you the corrected one, and I can enter it.\n- If the prescriber really wants this dose, they should enter or co-sign the order themselves, with a pharmacist review. I'm not the right route for an order like that.\n\nIf you give me a corrected dose from the prescriber, such as 800 mg by mouth four times daily (3,200 mg per day), I can enter it right away."
      }
